			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2220582">
									

<p>This comes down to simple headcanon, as far as the interview bit goes.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Expounding&nbsp;on&nbsp;headcanon&nbsp;time!</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;yayy</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>In interviews, I feel as if the Foundation has more freedom and leniency, both in-universe and out. Obviously, they have to treat these things as objects to contain, but there is room for humanity at some level. There are works on the site that manage to do this very well, treating the subject (provided the Foundation has no reason for hostility towards the skip, and reason to drop the clinical tone in-person) in interviews as not an object, but with some kind of humanity. So this is what I tried to do here.</p>
<p>Plus, figuring that she's nervous and scared and the Foundation would get better information out of her by treating her with some kind of level of humanity, that's what they opted to go for. Of course, if there's a problem with the interviewer's voice while going for that, I'm more than willing to listen to feedback on that.</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;yayy</a></div>
</div>
